{
  "title": "Redis vs Valkey vs Dragonfly: which in-memory store?",
  "content": "The failure is quiet — no errors, just wrong answers. Most rate limiting incidents trace back to a default nobody remembers choosing. Here's the root cause, not just the symptom.\n\nA typical scenario: a team running Redis 7 on a single node hits a memory wall. They need to add a replica, but the licensing change from Redis Ltd. (from BSD to RSALv2/SSPL) makes them pause. They've heard about Valkey, the Linux Foundation fork, and Dragonfly, a multithreaded rewrite. The question is not which is \"best\" — it's which one won't force a rewrite of their Lua scripts, their client library assumptions, or their operational runbooks. This post walks through the mechanisms that differ, the failure modes each introduces, and a decision framework you can apply to your own workload. The real problem this solves: choosing an in-memory store by benchmark numbers instead of by the specific compatibility and concurrency mechanisms that determine whether your existing code and ops practices survive the migration.\n\n## The fork in the road: what actually changed when Valkey forked Redis\n\nIn 2026, Redis Ltd. moved Redis from the BSD-3-Clause license to a dual RSALv2/SSPLv1 license. The Linux Foundation then forked the last BSD-licensed code (Redis 7.2.4) as Valkey. The important part for a developer choosing a store is not the politics — it's what that fork point implies.\n\nValkey starts from Redis 7.2.4. That means every command, every configuration directive, every module API that existed in 7.2.4 is present in Valkey by construction. If your application uses `SET key value EX 60 NX`, `XADD`, `EVAL`, or `CLIENT TRACKING`, Valkey implements it because the code is the same code. The divergence begins after the fork: Valkey has its own release cadence, its own new features (for example, changes to replication internals and new commands), and its own security patches.\n\nThe practical consequence: a Redis 7.2.4 client library will connect to Valkey and work. A Redis 7.4 client will also work for the commands that existed in 7.2.4, but any command added in Redis 7.4 (or later) will return an error against Valkey unless Valkey has independently added it. That is the compatibility line you need to draw for your own codebase: list the commands you actually call, and check them against the Valkey command reference, not against the Redis 7.4 reference.\n\nDragonfly takes a different path. It is a from-scratch implementation of the Redis protocol (RESP) and command set, designed around a shared-nothing, multithreaded architecture. It is not a fork; it reimplements the commands. That means compatibility is a matter of what Dragonfly has implemented, not what it inherited. The Dragonfly documentation maintains a compatibility page listing supported commands and known differences. For a team whose application uses only strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, and pub/sub, Dragonfly is likely a drop-in. For a team that relies on Lua scripting with `redis.call`, on modules, or on specific `DEBUG` subcommands, verify each one before committing.\n\nSo the first decision axis is not performance. It is: **how much of the Redis surface do you actually use, and which implementation covers that surface with the least behavioral risk?**\n\n## The mechanism that breaks first: single-threaded command execution under memory pressure\n\nRedis (and Valkey, which inherits the same event-loop design) executes commands on a single thread. That thread also handles client I/O, replication, and expiry. The design is simple and predictable: one command at a time, no locks on data structures. It is also the source of the most common production surprise.\n\nWhen a command is slow, everything behind it waits. Slow commands fall into a few categories:\n\n- **O(N) commands on large collections.** `KEYS *`, `SMEMBERS` on a set with millions of members, `HGETALL` on a huge hash, `ZRANGE` with a wide range, `SORT` without `LIMIT`, `LRANGE` over a long list. These block the event loop for the duration of the scan.\n- **Expiry storms.** If many keys share the same TTL (for example, a cache warm-up that sets `EX 3600` on a million keys at once), they all become eligible for expiry in the same second. Redis uses a lazy + active expiry strategy; the active cycle samples keys, but a large enough cohort can still produce latency spikes.\n- **Large `DEL` or `FLUSHALL`.** Deleting a big key frees memory incrementally but still runs on the main thread. `UNLINK` moves the free to a background thread and is the correct tool for large keys.\n- **Persistence forks.** `BGSAVE` and `BGREWRITEAOF` fork the process. On Linux, fork is copy-on-write, but the page-table copy itself is proportional to the size of the process's memory map. A 64 GB instance with millions of small allocations can take a noticeable pause at fork time, and writes during the save increase the copy-on-write footprint.\n\nA typical failure mode: p99 latency on `GET` jumps from sub-millisecond to tens of milliseconds for a few seconds, with no change in request rate. The cause is often a background save coinciding with a burst of writes, or a single `KEYS` call from an admin script. The fix is not a faster server; it is removing the blocking command and controlling persistence timing.\n\nDragonfly's architecture addresses this directly. It uses multiple threads, with each thread owning a shard of the keyspace, and it uses a different memory management approach (it advertises a \"dashtable\" structure for the hash table). The claim is that a single Dragonfly instance can use multiple cores, so a slow command on one shard does not necessarily block commands on another. That is a real architectural difference, and it matters if your workload is CPU-bound on the Redis side (for example, high-throughput `GET`/`SET` with large values, or heavy `EVAL` usage).\n\nBut the trade-off is behavioral compatibility. Multithreaded execution changes the atomicity guarantees you might be implicitly relying on. In Redis, a Lua script is atomic: no other command runs while it executes. In Dragonfly, the documentation states that scripts are executed atomically as well, but the underlying concurrency model means you should verify multi-key operations that span shards. Commands like `MGET` across keys that hash to different shards, or transactions (`MULTI`/`EXEC`) that touch multiple keys, may have different performance characteristics or, in edge cases, different semantics. The Dragonfly docs call out which commands are not supported or behave differently; read that list against your code.\n\nThe mechanism to internalize: **single-threaded execution gives you simple atomicity and predictable per-command latency, at the cost of head-of-line blocking. Multithreaded execution removes head-of-line blocking, at the cost of a more complex compatibility surface.** Neither is universally better; the question is which failure mode your workload can tolerate.\n\n## Picking by workload shape, not by benchmark chart\n\nBenchmarks for in-memory stores are notoriously sensitive to pipeline depth, value size, client count, and whether persistence is enabled. A `redis-benchmark` run with `-P 16` (pipeline 16) and no persistence will show very different numbers from a real application doing one round-trip per request with AOF enabled. Rather than quote a chart, use the following decision framework based on workload characteristics.\n\n| Workload characteristic | Redis (7.x) | Valkey | Dragonfly |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Existing Lua scripts using `redis.call` | Full support | Full support (fork of 7.2.4) | Supported, but verify atomicity semantics for multi-key scripts |\n| Redis modules (RediSearch, RedisJSON, etc.) | Supported via Redis Stack | Module ecosystem is nascent; check availability | Not supported; Dragonfly has its own search/JSON features with different APIs |\n| Single-node throughput bound by one core | Limited by single thread | Same as Redis | Scales across cores; primary reason to choose it |\n| Strict command-level compatibility with Redis 7.2.4 | Yes (it is Redis) | Yes (same code) | Mostly, with a documented compatibility list |\n| License | RSALv2/SSPLv1 (or commercial) | BSD-3-Clause | Apache 2.0 (check current license) |\n| Operational tooling (redis-cli, redis-exporter, managed offerings) | Broadest | Growing; many managed providers now offer Valkey | Narrower; check your cloud provider and observability stack |\n\nA few notes on reading that table. \"Module ecosystem\" is the sharpest differentiator: if your application depends on RediSearch for full-text search or RedisJSON for document storage, Valkey's module support depends on third-party builds, and Dragonfly does not implement the Redis module API. You would be rewriting those features. For many teams, that alone decides the question.\n\nIf your workload is a straightforward cache — `GET`/`SET` with TTLs, maybe some hashes and sorted sets — all three are viable. The tie-breakers become operational: does your cloud provider offer a managed version, does your existing monitoring (for example, a Prometheus exporter that scrapes `INFO`) work unchanged, and does your client library have any version-specific behavior?\n\nIf your workload is CPU-bound on the Redis side — high request rate with small values, or heavy use of Lua — Dragonfly's multithreading is the mechanism that matters. A single Redis instance uses one core for command execution regardless of how many cores the machine has. If `INFO cpu` shows `used_cpu_sys` and `used_cpu_user` for the main thread saturating a core while other cores idle, that is the signal that a multithreaded implementation could help. This is not a benchmark claim; it is a statement about the architecture, and you can verify it on your own instance by watching per-thread CPU with `top -H` or `pidstat -t`.\n\n## The migration that goes wrong: client assumptions and expiry semantics\n\nThe most common migration failure is not a command that returns an error. It is a command that returns a slightly different result, or a timing assumption that no longer holds.\n\nConsider expiry. Redis expires keys lazily on access and actively in a background cycle. The exact moment a key disappears is not guaranteed to be the TTL boundary; it is \"at or after\" the TTL. If your application uses the presence of a key as a lock (`SET lock:resource value NX EX 30`), you already depend on Redis's atomic set-with-expiry. All three implementations support this command, but the behavior of `TTL` immediately after setting can differ by a few milliseconds across implementations. Code that does `if ttl == -2: ...` to detect expiry is fragile; code that relies on the atomic `SET NX EX` returning nil when the lock is held is robust.\n\nA more subtle case: `SCAN` guarantees. Redis's `SCAN` provides a set of guarantees (elements present for the entire iteration are returned; elements never present are never returned) but does not guarantee a consistent snapshot. If you use `SCAN` to iterate keys for a background job, and you migrate to an implementation with different rehashing behavior, the iteration order and the number of calls can change. That is usually fine, but if your job assumes it will finish in a fixed number of `SCAN` calls, it may not.\n\nThe fix is to write a compatibility test suite that runs against all candidate stores. It does not need to be large. A minimal example in Python using `redis-py` (which works with Valkey and, for supported commands, Dragonfly):\n\n```python\nimport redis\nimport time\n\n# Connect to any RESP-compatible server\nr = redis.Redis(host=\"localhost\", port=6379, decode_responses=True)\n\ndef test_set_nx_ex():\n    r.delete(\"lock:test\")\n    assert r.set(\"lock:test\", \"a\", nx=True, ex=30) is True\n    assert r.set(\"lock:test\", \"b\", nx=True, ex=30) is None\n    ttl = r.ttl(\"lock:test\")\n    assert 0 < ttl <= 30, f\"unexpected ttl {ttl}\"\n    r.delete(\"lock:test\")\n\ndef test_scan_returns_all():\n    r.delete(\"scan:test:1\", \"scan:test:2\", \"scan:test:3\")\n    r.mset({\"scan:test:1\": \"x\", \"scan:test:2\": \"y\", \"scan:test:3\": \"z\"})\n    seen = set()\n    cursor = 0\n    while True:\n        cursor, keys = r.scan(cursor=cursor, match=\"scan:test:*\", count=10)\n        seen.update(keys)\n        if cursor == 0:\n            break\n    assert seen == {\"scan:test:1\", \"scan:test:2\", \"scan:test:3\"}, seen\n    r.delete(*seen)\n\ndef test_lua_atomicity():\n    r.delete(\"lua:counter\")\n    script = \"\"\"\n    local c = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\n    redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], 60)\n    return c\n    \"\"\"\n    assert r.eval(script, 1, \"lua:counter\") == 1\n    assert r.eval(script, 1, \"lua:counter\") == 2\n    r.delete(\"lua:counter\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    test_set_nx_ex()\n    test_scan_returns_all()\n    test_lua_atomicity()\n    print(\"compatibility checks passed\")\n```\n\nRun this against your current Redis instance, then against Valkey and Dragonfly. If a test fails, you have found a behavioral difference before it reaches production. The test is illustrative; extend it with the exact commands your application calls. You can extract that list by enabling command logging on a staging instance (for example, with `MONITOR` for a short window, or by reviewing your client library's command usage).\n\nOne more migration trap: replication and failover. Redis uses asynchronous replication by default. If you write to the primary and it fails before the write reaches the replica, the write is lost after failover. Valkey inherits this model. Dragonfly has its own replication implementation; check its documentation for the consistency guarantees and for how it handles failover. If your application uses `WAIT` to ensure a write reached N replicas, verify that `WAIT` is supported and behaves as expected on the target store. This is not a theoretical concern: a cache that loses writes on failover is usually acceptable, but a store used for session data or idempotency keys may not be.\n\n## What to watch after you switch\n\nOnce the new store is running, the metrics that matter are the ones that reveal the failure modes above. Set up alerts on these before cutover, not after.\n\n- **Latency percentiles, not averages.** Track p50, p99, and p999 for your command mix. A rise in p99 with flat p50 is the signature of head-of-line blocking or a slow command. On Dragonfly, watch for shard-level imbalance: if keys hash unevenly, one thread may saturate while others idle. `INFO` output and per-thread CPU are your sources.\n- **Command duration.** Redis exposes `latency` monitoring (`LATENCY LATEST`, `LATENCY HISTORY`) with a configurable threshold (`latency-monitor-threshold`). Set it to a value just above your expected p99 (for example, 10 ms) and alert on any event. This catches `KEYS`, large `DEL`, and slow scripts. Check whether the target store supports the same commands; if not, use client-side timing.\n- **Memory fragmentation and eviction.** `INFO memory` gives `used_memory`, `used_memory_rss`, and `mem_fragmentation_ratio`. A ratio well above 1 means the allocator is holding freed memory; a ratio below 1 means swapping, which is catastrophic for an in-memory store. Also watch `evicted_keys`: if it is rising, your `maxmemory-policy` is evicting data you may still need. The default policy in Redis is `noeviction`, which returns an error on writes when memory is full; many teams switch to `allkeys-lru` or `volatile-lru` for caches. Confirm the target store's default and supported policies.\n- **Persistence health.** `INFO persistence` gives `rdb_last_bgsave_status`, `aof_last_bgrewrite_status`, and `rdb_last_cow_size`. A failed background save is silent until you need to restart. Alert on non-`ok` status. If you use AOF with `appendfsync everysec`, you accept up to one second of writes lost on crash; that is a documented trade-off, not a bug.\n- **Replication lag.** `INFO replication` gives `master_repl_offset` on the primary and `slave_repl_offset` on replicas. The difference is the lag in bytes. A growing lag means the replica cannot keep up; failover to that replica loses more data. Alert if lag exceeds a threshold you derive from your write rate (for example, if you write 1 MB/s, a 10 MB lag is about 10 seconds of data at risk — illustrative arithmetic, not a measured figure).\n\nFor Dragonfly, the metric names and `INFO` sections may differ. Map each of the above to the equivalent in its documentation before you rely on a dashboard. A monitoring gap is a migration risk in itself.\n\n## The broader lesson: compatibility is a surface, not a checkbox\n\n\"Redis-compatible\" is not a binary property. It is a surface with layers: the wire protocol (RESP), the command set, the command semantics (atomicity, expiry timing, error codes), the module API, the replication model, and the operational tooling. Two stores can both be \"Redis-compatible\" and still differ on the layer your application depends on.\n\nThe principle: **choose the store whose incompatibilities you can enumerate.** Valkey's incompatibilities relative to Redis 7.2.4 are essentially zero at the fork point, and grow only as the two projects diverge. That is a small, trackable surface. Dragonfly's incompatibilities are documented in a list you can read in one sitting; the question is whether your application touches anything on that list. Redis's own incompatibilities relative to its past are the license and the feature additions in 7.4 and beyond. For each option, the risk is proportional to how much of the surface you use and how well you can test it.\n\nThis is also why benchmark-driven selection misleads. A throughput number tells you about the happy path under a specific load generator. It tells you nothing about whether your `EVAL` script that touches three keys is atomic, whether your `SCAN`-based job terminates, or whether your failover loses the last second of writes. Those are the things that page you at 3 a.m.\n\nA corollary: the migration test suite is more valuable than the migration plan. A plan lists steps; a test suite proves the steps worked. Write the tests first, run them against the current store to establish a baseline, then run them against each candidate. The candidate that passes is the one you can operate with confidence, regardless of what the benchmark chart says.\n\n## Start here: run the compatibility check against your current instance\n\nThe next 30 minutes: open a shell against a staging or local Redis instance and run `MONITOR` for 60 seconds while your application serves a representative workload, redirecting output to a file. Then extract the distinct command names (`awk '{print $3}' monitor.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn`) and compare that list against the Valkey command reference and the Dragonfly compatibility page. Any command that appears on your list but not on a candidate's supported list is a blocker to resolve before you consider that candidate. That single list turns the decision from a benchmark argument into a concrete, testable migration scope.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Redis documentation](https://redis.io/docs/)\n- [Prometheus documentation](https://prometheus.io/docs/)\n- [Google SRE Book: Monitoring Distributed Systems](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
